  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protection and resilience of migrants in situations of vulnerability affected by the conflict in the Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">According to the Government of Egypt&#x2019;s (GoE) Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MoFA), since the eruption of the conflict in Sudan in April 2023, 1.2 million refugees and migrants from Sudan have entered Egypt, seeking safety and security. Most arrive in dire conditions, separated from their families, distressed, and urgently needing lifesaving assistance. To respond to the crisis, IOM proposes to provide a comprehensive suite of services across the Humanitarian-Peace-Development Nexus, which will ensure that vulnerabilities exacerbated by the conflict are addressed, whilst enhancing self-reliance and strengthening social cohesion. The objective of this proposed project is to contribute to enhanced self-reliance and reduced vulnerabilities in vulnerable migrant communities fleeing the conflict in Sudan. Activities will mostly be implemented in Cairo, Alexandria, Aswan and other governorates hosting high numbers of crisis-affected Sudanese arrivals.</narrative>
  </description>
